epistle-panel for Debian
========================

This package installs a podup compose fragment, not the panel itself. The panel
runs only inside the container image ghcr.io/glyndor/epistle-panel, and the
fragment pins that image to the version of this package. `apt upgrade` is what
moves the panel to a new release.

What the package installs
-------------------------

  /usr/share/glyndor/epistle-panel/compose.yaml    the stack fragment (replaced on upgrade)
  /etc/glyndor/epistle-panel/                      configuration, owned by glyndor-epistle-panel
  /etc/glyndor/epistle-panel/secrets/              mode 0700, one file per secret, mode 0400
  /var/lib/glyndor/epistle-panel/                  state directory (the panel keeps none today)

The system account and group are glyndor-epistle-panel. Nothing is created
outside those paths.

Secrets
-------

The fragment mounts two files read-only as compose secrets under /run/secrets/.
They never appear in the compose file or in the container's environment, only
as file paths in EPISTLE_API_TOKEN_FILE and EPISTLE_PANEL_SESSION_SECRET_FILE.

  epistle_panel_session_secret  generated on first install (48 random bytes,
                                base64). An existing file is never overwritten,
                                so sessions survive upgrades.
  epistle_api_token             not generated. Put an epistle API token there,
                                mode 0400. Without it the stack cannot start.

Network
-------

The service publishes 127.0.0.1:7587 only. Put your own TLS proxy in front of it
to expose the panel. It joins the default network of the stack it is added to
and reaches the epistle API at http://mail:8025, a port that is not published.
To use another address, set EPISTLE_API_URL in a compose.override.yaml; do not
edit the installed fragment.

Removal
-------

`apt remove` keeps the account, the secrets and the state. `apt purge` deletes
/etc/glyndor/epistle-panel, /var/lib/glyndor/epistle-panel and the account.
